WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:15.910
Messier 21 or M21, also designated NGC 6531 or Webb's Cross, is an open cluster of stars located to the north-east of Sagittarius in the night sky, close to the Messier objects M20 to M25.

00:16.486 --> 00:21.823
It was discovered and catalogued by Charles Messier on June 5, 1764.

00:22.293 --> 00:25.346
This cluster is relatively young and tightly packed.

00:25.742 --> 00:33.406
A few blue giant stars have been identified in the cluster, but Messier 21 is composed mainly of small dim stars.

00:34.034 --> 00:44.650
With a magnitude of 6.5, M21 is not visible to the naked eye; however, with the smallest binoculars it can be easily spotted on a dark night.
